 robotics has driven significant research into autonomous systems, fully autonomous decision-making remains challenging. Consequently, human-in-the-loop (HITL) Robotics offers a viable solution by harnessing human expertise, knowledge, and input while minimizing the need for extensive data and computation. Within this framework, the capacity of a system to interact with and respond to human input becomes a pivotal capability for HITL, as learning from human expertise fosters collaboration between humans and robots, thereby facilitating the automation of complex tasks and enhancing human well-being through advanced robotic technologies. This paper introduces a novel data-driven approach for learning human responses within HITL, specifically tailored for learning from Counterfactual Human Inputs (CHIs). While previous studies have explored various forms of human inputs, including preferences, explanations, and corrections, CHIs represent a new, implicit, and non-intrusive method for providing system feedback. We define CHI as a counterfactual statement expressing what a system's failure would look like; for instance, if a system outputs "John broke the vase," a failure would be indicated by the counterfactual statement, "John did not break the vase." Data generated through such counterfactual interactions possesses the property that multiple inputs can be derived from a single failure, and these counterfactuals are effective at guiding system corrections. However, the non-factual nature of CHI data presents unique challenges for learning and utilizes HITL resources differently than previous approaches. To address these issues, we propose a new data representation and utilize Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) to learn it. This method enables the reconstruction of relevant information from CHI data, facilitates meaningful guidance for system correction, and allows for the simulation of virtual human inputs. We validate this approach on a learned robot policy, demonstrating its effectiveness. Additionally, we analyze the properties of both the recent CHI data and our proposed methods, showing that our approach surpasses existing alternatives. Our code is available at \url{https://github.com/zyy0123/gan-for-CHI}. \textbf{ARXIV COMMENT: We have updated the paper with a demonstration and analysis of the robot policy results. We have also added a section discussing the advantages of our method over previous work.}
